WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.990
When you send a photo to a friend, it

2
00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:04.310
doesn’t travel in one piece.

3
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.360
It’s chopped into pieces that can race

4
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.560
across the network by different roads.

5
00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:13.090
Today, let’s follow them, and meet the

6
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.630
people who guard those roads.

7
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.960
Today: how networks carry your messages,

8
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.380
and how defenders keep them safe.

9
00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.070
Everything you send is split

10
00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:25.010
into small packets.

11
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.860
Each packet carries two addresses,

12
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.680
called IP addresses: where it’s

13
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.080
from, and where it’s going.

14
00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:35.150
Routers pass it along, hop by

15
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.250
hop, and your friend’s phone puts

16
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.530
the pieces back together.

17
00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:42.410
There are many roads between two places.

18
00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.080
If a cable is cut or a router fails,

19
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.320
packets simply take another route.

20
00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.390
That’s why the internet keeps

21
00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.050
working when parts of it break.

22
00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.270
Defenders protect three things, and

23
00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.550
every attack hits at least one.

24
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.200
Confidentiality: only the

25
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.280
right people can read it.

26
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.980
Integrity: nobody can change it unnoticed.

27
00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.940
Availability: it works when you need it.

28
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:10.580
Quick check!

29
00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.110
Someone secretly changes a student’s

30
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.950
mark in the university’s records.

31
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.480
Which goal did they break?

32
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:20.700
Integrity.

33
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.440
Nobody needed to read the mark or

34
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.440
block the system: it was changed.

35
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.700
Defenders protect integrity with

36
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.900
fingerprints, signatures and

37
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.660
records of who changed what.

38
00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:36.950
No single defence is enough, so defenders

39
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.450
build layers, like an old castle.

40
00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.950
A wall, then a gate, then guards, then

41
00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.590
a locked room for the treasure.

42
00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.540
If one layer fails, the next still stands.

43
00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.350
On a network, the layers look like this.

44
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:55.710
A firewall decides which

45
00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.890
traffic may pass in and out.

46
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.520
The university’s records network is

47
00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.460
kept apart from students’ Wi-Fi.

48
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:04.940
Updates close known holes.

49
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.160
And spare routes and backups keep

50
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.800
things running when something fails.

51
00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.710
In banks, mobile money companies and

52
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.410
hospitals, a team called a security

53
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.350
operations centre watches the alarms day

54
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:19.850
and night.

55
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.140
They read logs: records of who

56
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.440
connected, when and from where.

57
00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.830
Then they spot what doesn’t fit,

58
00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.530
like a sign-in from another country

59
00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:31.610
at three in the morning.

60
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:33.660
Your turn.

61
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.040
On results day, the exam results

62
00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.520
website goes down for everyone.

63
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:41.570
Which goal is hit?

64
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.540
Availability: nothing was read or

65
00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.680
changed, but nobody could use it.

66
00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.310
Remember: packets hop along many routes,

67
00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.630
defenders guard confidentiality, integrity

68
00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:58.370
and availability, and they build layers so

69
00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.990
one failure isn’t the end.

70
00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.940
Try it tomorrow: in the lab, break a

71
00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.200
few sensors and add relays until

72
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.800
every message gets through.

73
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.220
Then draw your campus’s or your home’s

74
00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.640
network on paper, and mark where each

75
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.020
layer of defence would go.
